**Proof.**

Let $L_d := (\mathbb{C}^{n+1}\setminus 0)\times_{\mathbb{C}^*}\mathbb{C}$, where the $\mathbb{C}^*$-action is
$$t\cdot\bigl((z_0,\dots,z_n),\,w\bigr)=\bigl((tz_0,\dots,tz_n),\,t^d\,w\bigr).$$
The projection $\pi:L_d\to\mathbb{P}^n$ sends $[z,w]\mapsto[z]$. We show $L_d\cong\mathcal{O}(d)$ by computing transition functions on the standard open cover and matching them with those of $\mathcal{O}(d)$.

**Step 1: Standard open cover and local frames for $L_d$.**

Set $U_i=\{[z]\in\mathbb{P}^n:z_i\neq 0\}$ for $i=0,\dots,n$. On $U_i$, every point $[z]$ has a unique representative with $z_i=1$, namely $\widehat{z}=z/z_i$. Define a local frame
$$s_i([z]) \;=\; \bigl[\,\widehat{z},\;1\,\bigr] \;=\; \bigl[\,z/z_i,\;1\,\bigr]\;\in\; L_d\big|_{[z]}.$$
This is well-defined: choosing a different representative $tz$ gives $\widehat{tz}=tz/(tz_i)=z/z_i=\widehat{z}$, unchanged.

**Step 2: Local trivialization.**

Any element $[z,w]\in L_d$ over $[z]\in U_i$ can be rewritten using $t=z_i$:
$$[z,\,w]=\bigl[z_i\cdot(z/z_i),\,w\bigr]=\bigl[z/z_i,\;w/z_i^d\bigr]=\frac{w}{z_i^d}\cdot\bigl[z/z_i,\,1\bigr]=\frac{w}{z_i^d}\cdot s_i([z]).$$
Hence the $i$-th coordinate (trivialization $\tau_i:L_d|_{U_i}\to\mathbb{C}$) is
$$\tau_i\bigl([z,w]\bigr)=w/z_i^d.$$

**Step 3: Transition functions of $L_d$.**

On $U_i\cap U_j$, relate the two frames. Using $t=z_i/z_j$:
$$s_i([z])=\bigl[z/z_i,\,1\bigr]=\bigl[(z_i/z_j)(z/z_i),\,(z_i/z_j)^d\bigr]=\bigl[z/z_j,\,(z_i/z_j)^d\bigr]=\left(\frac{z_i}{z_j}\right)^d s_j([z]).$$
So $s_i=(z_i/z_j)^d\,s_j$. If $[z,w]=f_i\,s_i=f_j\,s_j$, then
$$f_j=\left(\frac{z_i}{z_j}\right)^d f_i.$$
Thus the transition function $g_{ij}^{L}$ (defined by $f_j=g_{ij}^{L}\,f_i$) is
$$\boxed{g_{ij}^{L}=\left(\frac{z_i}{z_j}\right)^d.}$$

**Step 4: Transition functions of $\mathcal{O}(d)$.**

Recall the tautological line bundle $\mathcal{O}(-1)$ has fiber $\ell_{[z]}=\mathbb{C}z\subset\mathbb{C}^{n+1}$ over $[z]$, with local frame $e_i([z])=z/z_i\in\ell_{[z]}$. Since $e_i=(z_j/z_i)\,e_j$, its transition functions are $g_{ij}^{\mathcal{O}(-1)}=z_j/z_i$.

The hyperplane bundle $\mathcal{O}(1)=\mathcal{O}(-1)^*$ has the inverse transition functions:
$$g_{ij}^{\mathcal{O}(1)}=\frac{z_i}{z_j}.$$
Equivalently, $\mathcal{O}(1)$'s sections on $U_i$ are generated by the homogeneous coordinate $x_i$ (degree-1), and $x_j=(x_j/x_i)\,x_i$ gives $g_{ij}^{\mathcal{O}(1)}=x_i/x_j=z_i/z_j$.

By tensor power, $\mathcal{O}(d)=\mathcal{O}(1)^{\otimes d}$ (for $d>0$; analogously for $d\leq 0$ via $\mathcal{O}(-1)^{\otimes|d|}$) has transition functions
$$g_{ij}^{\mathcal{O}(d)}=\left(\frac{z_i}{z_j}\right)^d.$$

**Step 5: Conclusion.**

The transition functions of $L_d$ and $\mathcal{O}(d)$ on the common open cover $\{U_i\}$ are identical:
$$g_{ij}^{L}=g_{ij}^{\mathcal{O}(d)}=\left(\frac{z_i}{z_j}\right)^d\quad\text{for all }i,j.$$

Two line bundles on $\mathbb{P}^n$ with the same transition functions (relative to the same open cover) are isomorphic. Concretely, the map
$$\Phi:L_d\longrightarrow\mathcal{O}(d),\qquad [z,w]\longmapsto \frac{w}{z_i^d}\cdot e_i^{\otimes d}\big|_{[z]}\quad\text{on }U_i$$
is well-defined globally (the local expressions glue because the transition functions match) and is a fiberwise $\mathbb{C}$-linear isomorphism covering the identity on $\mathbb{P}^n$.

Therefore
$$\left(\mathbb{C}^{n+1}\setminus 0\right)\times_{\mathbb{C}^*}\mathbb{C}\;\cong\;\mathcal{O}(d).$$

**Sanity check ($d=-1$):** With $d=-1$, $g_{ij}^{L}=z_j/z_i$, which is exactly the tautological bundle $\mathcal{O}(-1)$. Indeed, the map $[z,w]\mapsto w\,z\in\ell_{[z]}$ identifies $L_{-1}$ with $\mathcal{O}(-1)$, since $[(tz),t^{-1}w]\mapsto t^{-1}w\cdot(tz)=wz$. ✓

